            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Case Snapshot: A Successful Payment Does Not Mean the Books Match</h2>
<p>A cross-border e-commerce team advertises across Meta, Google, and TikTok, with some accounts funded through an agency. At the monthly close, finance finds that a consolidated bank debit does not match the platform invoices. Meanwhile, the media team notices that balances, promotional credits, and refunds are missing from the agency’s statement.</p>
<p>The figures below illustrate the reconciliation process and are not industry benchmarks:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Item</th>
<th style="text-align: right;">USD-equivalent Amount</th>
<th>Initial Assessment</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Bank debit during the period</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">86,200</td>
<td>Includes two payments belonging to the next billing cycle</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Platform-reported ad spend</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">85,400</td>
<td>Does not yet reflect account rebates</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Invoice amount</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">84,900</td>
<td>May be presented on a tax-exclusive basis</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Ending account balance</td>
<td style="text-align: right;">3,600</td>
<td>Includes promotional credits and unspent funds</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Putting the payment, spend, and invoice amounts into the same column does not resolve the difference. The first step is to distinguish three accounting views: <strong>cash movement, advertising consumption, and invoicing</strong>.</p>
<h2>Identify the Source of the Difference</h2>
<p>The team follows a structured review process:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Confirm the account scope.</strong> Match every ad account ID, currency, legal payer, and billing period. Separate platform-direct payments, agency-funded accounts, and credit-card autopayments.</li>
<li><strong>Check payment timing.</strong> The bank debit date may differ from the platform posting date. A payment crossing a month boundary belongs in a prepayment or in-transit schedule rather than current-period spend.</li>
<li><strong>Break down the charges.</strong> Distinguish media spend, platform fees, taxes, rebates, invalid-traffic refunds, and promotional credits. Credits are not cash deposits.</li>
<li><strong>Standardize exchange rates.</strong> Platforms may settle in local currency, while the bank charges in another currency. The team must select one consistent basis—such as payment-date, posting-date, or period-end exchange rates—and apply it throughout the close.</li>
<li><strong>Review invoice rules.</strong> Determine whether invoices include tax, and account for credit notes and adjustments. An agency invoice may also contain management fees and therefore should not be compared directly with media spend.</li>
</ol>
<p>In this case, the excess bank debit relates to the following month’s prepaid billing, platform spend has not yet been reduced by rebates, invoices use a tax-exclusive basis, and a small foreign-exchange difference comes from cross-currency postings.</p>
<h2>One Ledger Connecting Media and Finance</h2>
<p>Instead of retaining screenshots, the team creates three linked schedules.</p>
<h3>1. Payment and Funding Ledger</h3>
<p>Record the ad account, payer, payment currency, bank amount, transaction reference, platform-posted amount, posting date, and remaining cash balance. Split every consolidated payment across accounts using a documented allocation method.</p>
<h3>2. Advertising Spend Ledger</h3>
<p>Export daily platform reports covering media costs, service fees, taxes, rebates, refunds, and promotional-credit usage. Agency summaries should be sampled against platform records; the agency statement should not be the only source of truth.</p>
<h3>3. Invoice and Variance Ledger</h3>
<p>Link each invoice to a payment batch and ad account. Identify whether the amount is tax-inclusive or tax-exclusive, and record credit notes and adjustments. A variance should have a specific reason, such as a cross-month posting, exchange-rate rounding, pending rebate, invoice-basis difference, or billing error—not simply “balance mismatch.”</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Recommended reconciliation formula: Opening cash balance + Current-period funding ± In-transit adjustments - Current-period cash spend ± Refunds and rebates = Closing cash balance.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Exception-Handling SOP</h2>
<ul>
<li>If an amount falls within tolerance but remains unexplained, hold it as an open variance rather than forcing an adjustment.</li>
<li>If the agency statement conflicts with platform data, request the underlying invoices and sample the accounts directly.</li>
<li>If invoices are still outstanding, record the uninvoiced consumption to avoid period mismatches.</li>
<li>If a payment crosses the month-end, move it into the next reconciliation batch while retaining the bank and platform records.</li>
<li>Keep pending rebates and promotional credits separate from cash-funded spend.</li>
<li>If a payment fails or the balance is insufficient, pause new funding immediately and check account limits, payer entities, and payment methods. This prevents delivery disruptions from being discovered only after reconciliation.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Resolution and Monthly Review</h2>
<p>After adjustment, the case produces three traceable outcomes: the bank debit is matched to the relevant funding batches, including the amount moved to the next period; spend is recorded separately across media, fees, taxes, and rebates; and invoices align with the correct payer, billing period, and tax basis. The close no longer assumes that funding must equal spend. Instead, every variance has an owner, supporting evidence, and clear treatment.</p>
<p>The same process supports multi-account operations. The media team owns balances and consumption, finance owns payments and invoices, and the agency provides source billing support. Locking the data on a fixed monthly reconciliation date and clearing cross-month residuals early in the following month reduces spreadsheet rework and prevents incorrect adjustments.</p>
